# Curated golden evaluation set for the RAGAs harness (src/eval).
# One JSON object per line: {question, reference, subject?, level?}.
# `subject` must be a src.config.SUBJECTS label; `level` a src.config.LEVELS value.
# References are concise, factual ground-truth answers used only for context_recall.
{"question": "What is overfitting in machine learning and how can it be reduced?", "reference": "Overfitting is when a model learns the training data too closely, including its noise, so it performs well on training data but generalises poorly to unseen data. It is reduced with more or augmented training data, regularisation (L1/L2, dropout), simpler models, early stopping, and cross-validation.", "subject": "Machine Learning", "level": "Practitioner"}
{"question": "Explain the bias-variance tradeoff.", "reference": "The bias-variance tradeoff describes how a model's generalisation error splits into bias (error from overly simple assumptions that underfit) and variance (sensitivity to fluctuations in the training data that overfits). Increasing model complexity lowers bias but raises variance; the goal is the balance that minimises total expected error.", "subject": "Machine Learning", "level": "Practitioner"}
{"question": "How does the attention mechanism work in neural networks?", "reference": "Attention lets a model weigh the relevance of different input elements when producing each output. It computes queries, keys, and values; the similarity between a query and each key gives attention weights (via a softmax), and the output is the weighted sum of the values. This lets the model focus on the most relevant parts of the input regardless of distance.", "subject": "Deep Learning", "level": "Practitioner"}
{"question": "Describe the self-attention computation in the Transformer and why it scales quadratically.", "reference": "In Transformer self-attention each token is projected into query, key, and value vectors. Attention scores are the scaled dot products of queries with all keys, divided by the square root of the key dimension, passed through softmax, and used to weight the values. Because every token attends to every other token, computing the full attention matrix costs O(n^2) in sequence length n, which is the source of the quadratic scaling.", "subject": "Deep Learning", "level": "Researcher"}
{"question": "What is batch normalization and what problem does it address?", "reference": "Batch normalization normalises the activations of a layer across the mini-batch to zero mean and unit variance, then rescales and shifts them with learned parameters. It reduces internal covariate shift, stabilises and speeds up training, allows higher learning rates, and has a mild regularising effect.", "subject": "Deep Learning", "level": "Practitioner"}
{"question": "When should I use retrieval-augmented generation versus fine-tuning?", "reference": "Use RAG when knowledge changes often or must be cited, since it retrieves external documents at query time without retraining and keeps sources traceable. Use fine-tuning when you need to change the model's style, format, or behaviour, or teach stable domain skills. The two are complementary and are often combined.", "subject": "AI Engineering", "level": "Practitioner"}
{"question": "What is LoRA and why is it efficient for fine-tuning large language models?", "reference": "LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) freezes the pretrained weights and injects small trainable low-rank matrices into the layers, training only those. Because the added matrices have far fewer parameters than the full weights, it drastically cuts memory and compute, produces tiny checkpoints, and can be merged back or swapped per task.", "subject": "AI Engineering", "level": "Practitioner"}
{"question": "Explain reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF).", "reference": "RLHF aligns a language model with human preferences in three stages: supervised fine-tuning on demonstrations, training a reward model from human rankings of outputs, and then optimising the policy against that reward model with a reinforcement learning algorithm such as PPO. It steers models toward helpful, harmless, honest responses beyond what next-token prediction alone provides.", "subject": "AI Engineering", "level": "Practitioner"}
{"question": "What are embeddings and why are they useful?", "reference": "Embeddings are dense vector representations that map words, sentences, or other items into a continuous space where semantically similar items lie close together. They let models measure similarity numerically and power search, clustering, recommendation, and retrieval in RAG systems.", "subject": "Overlap (shared foundations)", "level": "Beginner"}
{"question": "What is gradient descent?", "reference": "Gradient descent is an optimisation algorithm that minimises a loss function by iteratively updating parameters in the direction opposite to the gradient of the loss. The step size is set by the learning rate. Common variants include batch, stochastic, and mini-batch gradient descent.", "subject": "Overlap (shared foundations)", "level": "Beginner"}
# Borderline foundations — legitimate ML/AI questions the conservative domain gate used to
# risk refusing (maths/statistics/classic-ML/evaluation). They must be answered, not declined.
{"question": "What is maximum likelihood estimation and how is it used to fit machine-learning models?", "reference": "Maximum likelihood estimation (MLE) chooses the model parameters that make the observed data most probable, i.e. that maximise the likelihood function. In practice one maximises the log-likelihood (or minimises the negative log-likelihood, which underlies losses like cross-entropy), often via gradient-based optimisation. Many standard models — logistic regression, Gaussian mixtures, neural-network classifiers — are trained by MLE.", "subject": "Machine Learning", "level": "Practitioner"}
{"question": "How does principal component analysis (PCA) reduce dimensionality?", "reference": "PCA finds an orthogonal set of directions (principal components) that capture the maximum variance in the data, computed from the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix (or via the SVD). Projecting the data onto the top-k components gives a lower-dimensional representation that retains most of the variance, which denoises data, speeds up downstream models, and aids visualisation.", "subject": "Machine Learning", "level": "Practitioner"}
{"question": "What is the difference between L1 and L2 regularization?", "reference": "Both add a penalty on weight magnitude to the loss to curb overfitting. L1 (lasso) penalises the sum of absolute weights, which drives many weights exactly to zero and yields sparse, feature-selecting models. L2 (ridge) penalises the sum of squared weights, which shrinks weights smoothly toward zero without eliminating them. Elastic Net combines the two.", "subject": "Machine Learning", "level": "Practitioner"}
{"question": "What do a ROC curve and its AUC tell you about a classifier?", "reference": "A ROC curve plots the true-positive rate against the false-positive rate as the decision threshold varies, showing the trade-off across all thresholds. The area under the curve (AUC) summarises it in one number: the probability that the classifier ranks a random positive above a random negative. AUC of 0.5 is random and 1.0 is perfect, and it is threshold-independent and robust to class imbalance.", "subject": "Machine Learning", "level": "Practitioner"}
{"question": "Why is the softmax function used to turn logits into probabilities?", "reference": "Softmax exponentiates each logit and normalises by the sum of the exponentials, producing values in (0, 1) that sum to one — a valid probability distribution over classes. The exponential keeps outputs positive and monotonic in the logits, and paired with cross-entropy loss it yields well-behaved gradients, which is why it is the standard output layer for multi-class classification.", "subject": "Deep Learning", "level": "Practitioner"}
